# Coding Conventions

## Naming
- Variables/methods: descriptive, verb-noun, camelCase
- Classes: noun, PascalCase
- Interfaces: I prefix (IUserRepository)
- Constants: SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE
- Avoid abbreviations (use `customerId`, not `custId`)

## Methods
- Max 30 lines; single responsibility
- Max 3 parameters; use DTO/record for more
- Return early; avoid deep nesting (max 2 levels)

## Constructors & Dependency Injection
- Prefer C# 12 Primary Constructors for classes and records with dependency injection (e.g. `public sealed class BookAppointmentHandler(ICustomerRepository customerRepository, ...)`) to eliminate redundant field declarations and boilerplate constructor assignments.
- Primary constructor parameters should use `camelCase` matching the dependency name.
- If a parameter must remain strictly immutable and protected from reassignment, or if complex constructor body logic is required, traditional constructors or private readonly field assignments may be used.

## Code Quality
- No magic numbers; use named constants
- No duplicated logic; extract to helper or base class
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Use expression-bodied members when single expression

## Readability
- One concept per line; no multiple statements
- Comment why, not what; delete dead code
- Order: fields → constructor → public → private
- ReSharper/IDE formatter enforced on save

## Logging
- Use source-generated logging with LoggerMessageAttribute (high-performance)
- Define partial class with static methods; source generator creates impl
- PascalCase placeholders: {Item}, {CustomerId}
- Avoid LogInformation/LogDebug; use LoggerMessageAttribute instead
- Check ILogger.IsEnabled before logging to skip allocations
- Log levels: Debug (dev), Information (ops), Warning (recoverable), Error/Critical (failures)

## Result & Error Handling
- Use `Result<T>` / `Result` with typed `ErrorKind` enum (e.g. `NotFound`, `ValidationFailed`, `Conflict`); never use raw string error codes
- Domain/Application layers return `Result.Failure(ErrorKind.X, "message")`; the message is for humans, the kind is for routing
- HTTP status mapping is centralized in `ResultMapper.ToStatus()` — endpoints must call `ResultMapper.ToStatus(result.ErrorKind)`, never inline switch/case on `ErrorCode`
- Endpoints must not branch on error code to set status; that logic lives in the mapper

